.TH SPOOR 1 "2026-09-12" "spoor 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spoor \- instant file-name search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spoor query
.RI [ options ] " pattern" ...
.RI [ -exec " command " [ argument ...]
.BR {} " " ;| + ]
.br
.B spoor gui
.RI [ --socket
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B spoor daemon
.RI [ --config
.IR PATH "] [" --root " " DIR ]...
.br
.B spoor configure
.RI [ FILE | - ]
.br
.B spoor stats \fR|\fB bench \fR|\fB krunner \fR|\fB selftest \fR|\fB --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spoor
answers file-name searches instantly, in the spirit of Everything on
Windows. A root daemon keeps an index of the chosen folders current through
fanotify, and serves it over a Unix socket to a command-line client, a GTK
window and a KDE KRunner plugin.
.PP
Because the daemon runs as root, every reply is filtered by the asking
user's own permissions: a result is shown only if that user could list the
folder holding it. The check is made by the kernel, under the caller's uid,
gid and groups, taken from the socket rather than from the request.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B query \fIpattern\fR...
Print matching paths, one per line, as raw bytes. Several words are ANDed and
each may match anywhere in the full path; double quotes keep a phrase together.
.TP
.B gui
The search window. Also available as "File Search" in the application menu.
.TP
.B daemon
The indexing service, normally started by systemd, not by hand.
.TP
.B configure \fR[\fIFILE\fR|\fB-\fR]
Validate settings and install them in
.IR /etc/spoor/spoor.conf ,
then restart the service. Root only; the window's Preferences run it through
pkexec. With
.B -
or no argument the settings are read from standard input.
.TP
.B stats
Entry and arena counts of the running index.
.TP
.B bench \fIpattern\fR...
Time queries server-side, without process start-up in the number.
.TP
.B selftest \fR[\fB--root \fIDIR\fR]
Prove live indexing: watch, walk, then create a deep new directory tree and
confirm the file inside it becomes searchable. Root only.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Search options
.TP
.B --case
Match case. Otherwise case is ignored, in every script.
.TP
.B --regex
Treat the whole query as a regular expression.
.TP
.B --path
Match against the full path, not just the name. A
.B /
in the query does this anyway; a trailing
.B /
means "inside that folder".
.TP
.BR --files ", " --folders
Restrict results by type.
.TP
.B --no-hidden
Leave out dot-files and everything inside dot-directories.
.TP
.BI --limit " N"
At most N results (default 100).
.TP
.B --null
End each result with a NUL instead of a newline, for names containing
newlines. Pairs with
.BR "xargs -0" .
.TP
.BI -exec " command " [ argument "...] {} " ;
Run the command on each result instead of printing it, with
.B {}
replaced by the name, as in
.BR find (1).
The command is started directly, never through a shell, so a name holding
spaces, quotes or newlines needs no escaping. Note that the shell eats a bare
semicolon: write
.BR \e; .
.TP
.BI -exec " command " [ argument "...] {} " +
The same, but one run carrying as many names as a command line holds, which is
much faster for many results. The
.B {}
must come last. Unlike
.BR find (1),
spoor exits non-zero if any run of the command failed.
.IP
With
.BR -exec ,
every match is acted on rather than the first 100; pass
.B --limit
to bound it.
.SS Daemon options
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Settings file (default
.IR /etc/spoor/spoor.conf ).
.TP
.BI --root " DIR"
Index DIR. Repeatable; adds to the settings file.
.TP
.BI --exclude " DIR"
Leave DIR out, with everything beneath it. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI --rescan " DIR"
Walk DIR on a timer instead of watching it, for mounts fanotify cannot see
(rclone, network filesystems). Repeatable.
.TP
.BI --rescan-interval " SECS"
Seconds between those walks (default 900).
.TP
.BI --reconcile-interval " SECS"
Seconds between full reconciliation walks (default 86400, 0 to switch off).
.TP
.BI --socket " PATH"
Socket to serve on (default
.IR /run/spoor.sock ).
.TP
.BI --state " PATH"
Snapshot file (default
.IR /var/lib/spoor/index.bin ).
.TP
.BI --save-interval " SECS"
Seconds between snapshots (default 60, 0 to switch off).
.TP
.B --no-watch
Serve a static index, without fanotify; needs no privileges.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/spoor/spoor.conf
Which folders are indexed, excluded and rescanned. One
.I key = value
per line;
.IR root ", " exclude " and " rescan
may repeat.
.TP
.I /var/lib/spoor/index.bin
Snapshot of the index, readable by root only.
.TP
.I ~/.config/spoor/gui.conf
The window's own settings.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B spoor query invoice
Names containing "invoice".
.TP
.B spoor query wedding jpg
Both words, anywhere in the path.
.TP
.B spoor query --null invoice | xargs -0 ls -l
Names that may contain anything at all.
.TP
.B spoor query --regex '^IMG_[0-9]+\e.jpg$'
A regular expression over names.
.TP
.B spoor query invoice -exec cp {} ~/backup/ \e;
Copy every match, one run of cp per file.
.TP
.B spoor query .tmp -exec rm {} +
Remove every match, in as few runs of rm as possible.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR locate (1),
.BR updatedb (8),
.BR fanotify (7)
